{"text": "Restaurant suggestions in Italy\nby Louis Jacobson, with Elisabeth Layton\n\nRistorante Piperno\nMonte de Cenci, 9\nPhone 06.68806629\nFax 06.68219595\nWeb http://www. euro2001.com/piperno\nE-mail piperno@euro2001.com\nClosed all day Monday and dinner on Sunday\n\nThis was probably our favorite restaurant in Rome. It's located in\nRome's old Jewish quarter, among a maze of cobblestoned streets.\n(For visitors unfamiliar with Rome, it pays to take a taxi.) Piperno\noffers a small, wood-paneled dining room and excellent service. And\nthe food is great. The carciofi alla giudia (whole fried artichokes)\nare Piperno's specialty, and they're breathtakingly good: paper-thin\nleaves seared to a crisp, with hearts that are soft and warm, but with\nvery little residual grease. In the same vein, the fiori di zucca ripieni\ne fritti (fried zucchini flowers) are also memorable. Both dishes are\ntypically served as appetizers. We then had cuttlefish and a stew of\negg noodles and chick peas, both of which were very good. For\ndessert, they served us fresh, adorably small wild strawberries over\nvanilla ice cream. Lunch with dessert cost about $35 a person\nwithout wine; dinner would likely be more.\n\nVecchia Roma",
